TITLE: DESIGN OF A HYBRID INFORMATION SECURITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM BASED ON ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE TECHNOLOGIES
TEXT: Information and controlling system The process of managing cybersecurity operations that include continuous monitoring, detection, analysis, and response to cyber threats, aimed at protecting the organization's digital assets, has been investigated in this study. The task addressed is to improve the efficiency and speed of automated incident response by combining deterministic pre-filtering tools with the cognitive capabilities of language models (LLMs). In the course of the research, an architecture of a multi-agent information security management system (MAS-ISM) has been designed, which operates on the basis of decentralized coalition auctions and a hybrid risk analysis subsystem (Sandwich Engine) for express search of incidents in system event logs for automated generation of expert reports in the state language using the generation technology supplemented by Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and LLM search. The solution to the tasks of obtaining technical cybersecurity reports in the state language and recommendations for further decision-making by the analyst has been shown. The main difference of the hybrid risk assessment system from existing ones is the use of deterministic retrospective session isolation, which made it possible to ensure data confidentiality, automatic expert description of the current state of cybersecurity in the national language, and the compilation of recommendations for analysts. The research results are attributed to the combined use of AI methods: RAG technology, deterministic retrospective session isolation, and LLM. The total time for analyzing one complex incident and forming a structured expert SOC report in the state language was reduced to 12 seconds (compared to 15–45 minutes under traditional manual analysis), while guaranteeing the confidentiality of critical data from leakage into the public cloud circuit Keywords: cybersecurity, pre-filtration, LLM, multi-agent systems, event logs, Retrieval-Augmented Generation, hybrid systems UDC 004.056:004.896:510.644 DOI: 10.15587/1729-4061.2026.368661 DESIGN OF A HYBRID INFORMATION SECURITY MANAGEMENT SYSTEM BASED ON ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE TECHNOLOGIES Mykhailo Mozhaiev Doctor of Technical Sciences, Senior Researcher, Associate Professor Department of Electronic Computers Kharkiv National University of Radioelectronics Nauky ave., 14, Kharkiv, Ukraine, 61166 ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1566-9260 Inna Khavina Candidate of Technical Sciences, Associate Professor* ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1856-1186 Oleksandr Mozhaiev Corresponding author Doctor of Technical Sciences, Professor* E-mail: mozhaev1957@gmail.com ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1412-2696 Volodymyr Strukov Candidate of Technical Sciences, Associate Professor Department of Mathematical Modeling and Data Analysis V. N. Karazin Kharkiv National University Svobody sq., 4, Kharkiv, Ukraine, 61022 ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4722-3159 Yurii Gnusov Candidate of Technical Sciences, Associate Professor* ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9017-9635 Mykhailo Tsuranov Senior Lecturer* ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2115-7029 *Department of Cyber Security and DATA Technologies Kharkiv National University of Internal Affairs L. Landau ave., 27, Kharkiv, Ukraine, 61080 Received 12.05.2026 Received in revised form 20.07.2026 Accepted date 29.07.2026 Published date 28.08.2026 How to Cite: Mozhaiev, O., Khavina, I., Strukov, V., Mozhaiev, M., Gnusov, Y., Tsuranov, M. (2026). Development of a hybrid information security management system based on artificial intelligence technologies. Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Technologies, 4 (9 (142)), 33–46. https://doi.org/10.15587/1729-4061.2026.368661 1. Introduction The modern world is heavily dependent on information technology, which makes cybersecurity extremely important [1]. Various cyberattacks are becoming increasingly complex, threatening both individual users and entire organizations. The current cyberthreat landscape is characterized by a rapid increase in the complexity and intensity of attacks, in particular, zero-day threats and combined fan intrusions. Automated systems for managing information system (IS) events, SIEM systems, are used to solve the task of controlling the flow of events coming from integrated security control systems and to computerize the incident management process. These systems require frequent tuning and adaptation to fully and correctly perform their tasks, as they operate 33 Copyright © 2026 Authors. This is an open access article under the Creative Commons CC BY license Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Technologies ISSN-L 1729-3774; E-ISSN 1729-4061 4/9 ( 142 ) 2026 in a constantly changing, dynamic environment. They collect events in real time, analyze them in near real time, send notifications and display information on the operator console for faster implementation of protective measures. SIEM systems play a critical role in the diverse workflows of analysts at Security Operations Centers (SOCs). However, modern SIEM and AIOps platforms that use machine learning and Big Data to optimize IT operations face a major challenge: they still cannot free analysts from the constant tasks of analyzing and writing technical reports in natural languages and significant incident response times. State-of-the-art LLMs have demonstrated significant capabilities in interpreting unstructured data, generating threat descriptions, and even interacting with security environments through thought chains and augmented search generation. Big language models, using data sets and advanced neural network architectures, have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in understanding and generating human language. Thus, the use of big language models (LLMs) fundamentally changes approaches to cybersecurity, allowing for the analysis of unstructured data sets and the interpretation of the context of events directly in real time. However, despite the high analytical potential of LLMs, their direct application in response systems is limited by the high cost of computing resources, the “black box” issue, and the lack of mechanisms for a clear mathematical justification of decisions made (explainability of the result). Therefore, it is urgent to build a hybrid information security management system based on artificial intelligence technologies using a synergy of deterministic analysis and stochastic modeling methods. Such a system could reduce the time for incident analysis, fully ensure data confidentiality, as well as significantly reduce the risk of artificial intelligence errors (hallucinations) when using the state language. The hybrid approach involves the infrastructural and algorithmic combination of the following: – a local contour of the protected perimeter, which, based on deterministic mathematical logic, the TF-IDF algorithm, and finite automata, performs express search, information “noise” filtering, and two-way irreversible anonymization of SIEM system event logs; – an external analytical contour, which uses the cognitive capabilities of Transformer class models exclusively as a linguistic processor to generalize the semantic skeleton of the attack and generate expert reports in the state language in accordance with the requirements of the laws of Ukraine “On ensuring the functioning of the Ukrainian language as the state language” and “On the basic principles of ensuring cybersecurity of Ukraine”. In our work, this approach is implemented by combining the analytical capabilities of neural networks with the decentralized logic of multi-agent systems (MASs) and deterministic mathematical models for choosing optimal protection strategies. 2. Literature review and problem statement The integration of LLM agents into already known systems, including SIEM systems and AI-Ops platforms, is considered in [2]; the work allows us to draw further conclusions about the possibilities of building proactive cybersecurity systems. However, issues such as attacks and interpretability (explainability) of results are still unresolved. At the same time, the direct involvement of LLM in incident response circuits is limited by the significant cost of computing resources, the opacity of decision-making (the “black box” effect), and the lack of tools for their clear mathematical justification. To eliminate these limitations, researchers have turned to multi-agent architectures in the field of cybersecurity. Such systems have decentralized management based on software intelligent agents (SAs), which contain the logic for detecting specific events and each is responsible for analyzing a certain level of threat. This architecture provides scalability, resilience, and parallel processing capabilities, easily integrates into SIEM, and operates in parallel with the system. In [3], the evolution and application of various LLMs are analyzed. The tools used by LLM agents for the cybersecurity industry are considered, such as natural language processing for log analysis, contextual anomaly detection, pattern identification in network traffic, as well as user behavior analysis. It is noted that the use of LLMs is not possible due to data protection and user privacy laws. To work effectively, these systems require high-performance hardware and large memory capacity in terms of RAM and disks, which consumes a lot of energy, which can be an obstacle to the implementation of these systems, especially at small organizations. In [4], a modular multi-agent architecture is described that integrates established cybersecurity analysis tools with large language models (LLMs) to achieve intelligent, understandable, and highly accurate threat detection across a variety of data types. It is found that the underlying framework is a contextual recommendation system that processes and cross-analyzes the outputs of all specialized agents to detect complex threat patterns, such as multi-vector, temporal, or stealth attacks, that would otherwise evade isolated detection mechanisms. The paper does not address the optimality of protection, does not address the issue of state language reporting; data confidentiality may be compromised when using LLMs directly. A review work [5] explores the integration of LLMs into key cybersecurity tasks such as threat detection, and discusses ethical and technical issues, including privacy vulnerabilities, bias, and attacks on LLM applications. The fundamental role of LLM models in improving cybersecurity is emphasized. The lack of practical examples of solving problems is a drawback of the cited work. In [6], a multi-agent network intrusion detection system with a distributed (multi-sensor) architecture is proposed. This framework uses deep anomaly detectors to adapt each agent to changing attack patterns/benign anomalies in its local traffic. A federated learning approach is proposed to share local knowledge between different agents. However, the work does not show a practical solution for creating collective knowledge about attacks based on observations from different sensors. Review [7] examines how LLMs positively impact security and privacy, the potential risks and threats associated with their use, as well as the inherent vulnerabilities in LLMs. It was found that research on the impact of model architecture on the security of LLMs themselves is lacking. This may be due to high computational costs. Today, LLMs do not have direct access to OS and hardware-level functions. Potential threats to LLMs could increase if they were to gain such access. Study [8] highlights the broad potential of LLMs in improving cybersecurity practices. A review of over 150 studies on the application of LLMs to various cybersecurity tasks is given, of which about 35 considered threat detection and 34 Information and controlling system only 6 – log-based anomaly detection. The disadvantage is that they perform semantic analysis on the entire array of unstructured logs, which causes the problem of information overload. The likely reason is that the capabilities of the LLM model are currently under investigation. Processing performance was increased by using the ForenSift platform developed in [9], which applies retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and LLMs trained on the organization’s datasets for intelligent and adaptive digital evidence processing. It is found that, unlike all traditional log parsing methods, their method is based on clustering and vector embedding to detect anomalies in raw logs but, as practice has shown, the performance of the security system is low. The likely reason is poor clustering of raw logs. Also, parallel processing of streams may not be provided, which could improve response time. For example, paper [10] gives a literature review that discusses in detail the use of LLM for both malware development and detection. It is found that although LLM has great potential, LLM is not a panacea that can prevent or stop every type of malware attack. The disadvantage is that they perform semantic analysis on the entire array of unstructured logs, which causes the problem of information overload of the model. Study [11] investigates anomaly detection based on system logs, which is a major problem in AIOps as logs provide detailed evidence of failures, performance regressions, and security incidents. It is found that a trained two-layer transformer encoder with masked speech modeling only on regular sessions and anomaly estimation using pseudo-log likelihood is used. But there are drawbacks: external validity (over time, knowledge of the model becomes irrelevant), high cost of inference, heterogeneity of anomalies – over time it is increasingly difficult to detect this method, since the anomaly signal can be diluted by averaging tokens. In [12], the reasoning ability of thirteen LLMs was investigated, which were tested on two datasets of long text analogies. That required ranking a series of stories from most similar to least similar compared to a reference story. The three best LLMs were shown to be GPT-4, GPT-4o, and Gemini, respectively. The drawback was found that LLMs have difficulties with excessive emphasis on similar plot entities (characters and locations) and insufficient awareness of higher-order connections between stories; therefore, to obtain a deterministic answer, the text (raw data) must be filtered. Paper [13] provides information on the automation of report writing, which is a critical aspect of digital forensics investigations. It is found that, in combination with careful proofreading and corrections, LLMs can help practitioners during the report writing process. The disadvantage is the lack of a practical solution, and the work does not consider the generation of reports in the state language. Study [14] describes a decentralized auction-based algorithm for solving dynamic task allocation problems for spatially distributed multi-agent systems. The task allocation is dynamic because it is updated at discrete time steps to take into account the current states of the agents. A greedy coalition auction algorithm is implemented in terms of performance and computation time. The disadvantage of the approach is that when using large MACs, some scalability issues arise due to computation time. Work [15] considers multi-agent cooperative learning systems in which several agents act together to perform a common task. It is found that for the optimal operation of the coalition of agents, a regret minimization algorithm is used. Such an algorithm has not been used in the field of cybersecurity but, given the results reported in the work, it has prospects. In study [16], a solution to the task of optimal robot control is suggested. It was found that the solution was obtained based on a multi-agent system with decentralized control using communication between system agents using an auction protocol. In the field of cybersecurity, such an approach has not been used but, given the results reported in the study, it also has prospects. In [17], to address the challenge of capturing dynamic user behavior and complex interactions in social network content distribution, a method is proposed that captures the spatiotemporal evolution of user behavior, providing a technical approach to personalized recommendations and content resource allocation on social media platforms. The disadvantage is the complexity of applying the method because there are simpler approaches to enriching data on the actions of IS users in space and time. In [18], LLMs with different architectures are investigated, which are tested for their ability to better analyze system log files for security. It was found that the models demonstrate high efficiency in log analysis. The disadvantage is that they require constant fine-tuning, which is especially important for the appropriate adaptation of the domain to certain types of journals. Paper [19] explores the three-way basis of RAG frameworks, which includes search, generation, and addition methods. The disadvantage is the lack of an example of a practical solution. In [20], a report on the shortcomings of RAG systems is given. Seven shortcomings are identified that should be considered when designing the system. The disadvantage of the work is the lack of practical examples of solving problems and the conclusions are of a recommendatory nature. In [21], a modular and extensible agent-based RAG platform is described. It is found that these agents use LLMs as the core of reasoning and planning to determine the flow of program control, while maintaining the characteristics of traditional AI agents. The model evaluates the adequacy of its results and determines whether additional processing is required. However, the effectiveness of CyberRAG directly depends on the performance of the previous intrusion/threat detection system and does not allow for the detection of new types of attacks or attacks that it has not been trained to recognize. Our review of the literature [1–13] allowed us to identify a number of unresolved parts of the general problem that hinder the effective implementation of artificial intelligence in the field of cybersecurity. The general drawback is that semantic analysis is performed either on the entire array of unstructured logs, which causes the problem of information overload, or on isolated anomalous events, which leads to the loss of the tactical chain of attack. In the above studies on multi-agent systems (MASs), for example [14], it is noted that in large MASs, in which there are more than 10,000 agents, scalability issues arise due to the long calculation time. Methods for managing coalitions of agents are provided in [15–16]. A method for ascertaining the spatial-temporal evolution of user behavior is reported in study [17]. In [18–21], direct transfer of unstructured SIEM log arrays to cloud neural network models leads to an exponen- 35 Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Tech [...] em event logs and automated generation of expert reports in the state language using LLM (Fig. 3–5). A feature of the Sandwich Engine subsystem is that it uses RAG technology, deterministic retrospective isolation of sessions, and is built as a three-level architecture – the “sandwich” concept, which improved the runtime and stability of the content and quality of technical reports in the state language (Fig. 6, 7), (Table 1), in contrast to the RAGs reported in [2–13, 18–21]. That has made it possible to implement express search of anomalies in system event logs. The stability of the system’s response time to cybersecurity incidents (the average analysis value is 11–15 seconds) (Fig. 7, Table 1) is justified by the integration of the deterministic contour. The use of the TF-IDF algorithm (1), cosine similarity metrics (4), and the interaction between the deterministic and stochastic contours of the Sandwich Engine system prompt Psys (10) allowed us to reduce the computational complexity of the LLM model. The system cuts off > 99.9% of the information noise of the logs at the local level of the organization, transmitting to the stochastic contour of the gemini-2.5-flash model an exclusively concentrated semantic package of 6 critical events ( python_built_timeline ). Unlike solutions in [18–21], the generation of false facts of “hallucinations” by the model in our work is absent due to the deterministic retrospective isolation of sessions (explanation to Fig. 3). Since the input context of the AI is forcibly limited to a chronological slice of the actions of a specific attacker (for example, the j_doe account), the space for stochastic search of weights in the latent space of the neural network is narrowed. The model functions as a cognitive interpreter. In this case, data security requirements (ISO/IEC 27001) are ensured by local masking of confidential markers until the data is sent to the external API (8) to (9). The effectiveness of the LLM as a risk analysis subsystem that processes log files and alerts has also been evaluated (11). Within this architecture, the model provides SOC analysts with relevant instructions and action algorithms. For experimental verification of the ability of LLM to analyze the security status of an information system based on event logs, specialized software was developed – a cyberattack emulator. Despite its high efficiency, the designed subsystem has a number of limitations established during the experiments, which outline the limits of its application: – staticity of the input stream: empirical verification was carried out on fixed file samples, and not on a real streaming stream of network traffic (Streaming Data); – linearity of session binding: the current version of the Sandwich Engine prototype is adapted for sequential analysis of an incident within one compromised identifier. Complex scenarios of horizontal movement of a hacker with the parallel use of more than 5 different accounts require additional iterations of calculations. An important advantage of the designed hybrid architecture is the automatic overcoming of the language barrier when interpreting international cybersecurity standards (for example, NIST SP 800-61). The system prompt contains a strict linguistic restriction that forces the stochastic LLM circuit to generate the final expert report exclusively in the state language. At the same time, selective masking of technical markers is implemented: unique incident identifiers are copied from the cleaned timeline without linguistic translation. This allows SOC center operators to instantly receive legally and technically correct analytical cybersecurity reports in Ukrainian, fully ready for integration into national incident databases. As a drawback of this study, it should be noted that our work did not consider the issue of protecting the designed system itself. In particular, the tasks of ensuring the integrity of local TF-IDF indexes and protecting API keys to access the cloud circuit from compromise remained outside the scope of our experiment. The following may be tackled in further research. Integration of cryptographic means of protecting local memory (token and index storage) and implementation of dynamic secret management mechanisms for secure storage and rotation of Google GenAI SDK API keys, which would completely prevent their leakage. Modification of the subsystem code to ensure full compatibility with lightweight local models (for example, Llama-3-8B-Instruct or Mistral-7B) via the local Ollama server. This would allow the system to be deployed in fully isolated closed networks without the need for an external Internet 44 Information and controlling system connection, which could ensure full compliance with the requirements of domestic TZI standards and the international ISO/IEC 27001 standard. authorship, or any other, that could affect the study and the results reported in this paper. 7. Conclusions 1. We have proposed and formalized an architecture of a hybrid information security management system based on the MAS-ISM multi-agent system. A feature is the use of decentralized coalition auction algorithms for coordinating autonomous agents (threats, resources, and protection mechanisms) in real time. This allowed us to transfer the process of choosing a protection strategy from a reactive circuit to a proactive one and ensure mathematically justified optimization of the composition of security tools. 2. The Sandwich Engine risk analysis subsystem has been designed and experimentally verified, which implements the technology of local search-augmented generation, deterministic retrospective isolation of sessions, and LLM. The following quantitative indicators of the solution’s effectiveness were established: – the implementation of local regular grammars and the TF-IDF algorithm provides a compression and filtering coefficient of information “noise” of legitimate events at the level of 99.75%; – the volume of security context input data was reduced from 10,000 lines (~300,000 tokens) to 25 lines (~750 tokens), which reduced the load on the limited LLM context window by 400 times and completely eliminated the risk of model “hallucinations”; – the total time for automated analysis of one complex incident and generation of a structured expert SOC report in the state language was reduced to 12 seconds (compared to 15–45 minutes under traditional manual analysis), while the two-way data masking architecture guarantees confidentiality and protection of critical data from leakage into the public cloud circuit. Conflicts of interest The authors declare that they have no conflicts of interest in relation to the current study, including financial, personal, Funding The study was conducted without financial support. Data availability All data are available in the main text of the manuscript. Use of artificial intelligence The authors confirm that they used artificial intelligence technologies when preparing this manuscript because the work considers analysis of AI capabilities. For the generation and final debugging of the program code, the Gemini 2.5 flash language model toolkit from Google was used: – to search for information sources and partially for text translation; – to debug the system program code; – the authors checked the results – Khavina Inna, Tsuranov Mykhailo; – computer experiments confirmed the operability of the approach and showed sufficient speed and quality of the system. Author contributions Oleksandr Mozhaiev: Methodology; Inna Khavina: Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Supervision, Validation; Volodymyr Strukov: Supervision; Mykhailo Mozhaiev: Validation, Data Curation; Yurii Gnusov: Writing – review & editing; Mykhailo Tsuranov: Software, Writing – original draft. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the article and are jointly responsible for the reliability, accuracy, and integrity of the data and research results presented in the article.

